Handling Dual Date Roles

To model both Invoice Date and Delivery Date without introducing circular relationships or redundant table duplicates:

Active Relationship: Established between Dim_Date[Date] and Fact_Sales[InvoiceDate].
Inactive Relationship: Established between Dim_Date[Date] and Fact_Sales[DeliveryDate].
DAX Implementation: Leveraged the USERELATIONSHIP function within targeted measures to dynamically evaluate delivery performance without distorting standard invoice-based time intelligence.
Key Performance Metrics & DAX Analytics

Core KPIs Summary

Total Sales: 172.3 M
Gross Profit: 85.73 M
Total Cost: 86.53 M
Total Quantity Sold: 24 M
Overall YoY Growth: 15%
Key DAX Calculations (Sample Logic)

Total Sales

Total Sales = SUM(Fact_Sales[Sales_Amount])

Gross Profit

Gross Profit = [Total Sales] - SUM(Fact_Sales[Total_Cost])

YoY Sales Growth (%)

Sales YoY Growth % =

VAR CurrentYearSales = [Total Sales]

VAR PriorYearSales = CALCULATE([Total Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR(Dim_Date[Date]))

RETURN

DIVIDE(CurrentYearSales - PriorYearSales, PriorYearSales, 0)

Delivery-Based Sales (Inactive Relationship Execution)

Sales by Delivery Date =

CALCULATE(

[Total Sales],

USERELATIONSHIP(Fact_Sales[Delivery_Date], Dim_Date[Date])

)

Strategic Recommendations

Customer Retention Framework: Implement an account management strategy for high-value client cohorts. Address the decay rate of top-tier accounts to secure baseline recurring revenue.
Mitigating Seasonal Dips: Design promotional campaigns targeted specifically at mid-Q1 (February) and mid-Q3 (August) to smooth out intra-year volatility.
Geographic Scaling: Double down on successful expansion protocols currently driving strong yield in top regions like Texas, replicating these playbooks across lower-performing states.
Technical Stack

ETL & Data Transformation: Power Query (M Language)
Data Modeling & Analytics: Power BI Desktop, DAX (Data Analysis Expressions)
Architecture: Star Schema Methodology
